#
# A syllable holds a vowel. These four say so, and between them they replace
# some fifty single-word repairs that would otherwise be needed one at a time.
#
# An empty first piece: rule 11 puts its break before the match, and where the
# match opens the line there is nothing in front of it. Mid-line the double
# space above already swallowed it, which is why this was only ever visible on
# a line beginning "blessed".
s/^ -- //g;
# A final cluster with no vowel is not a syllable: "hy -- mn", "Lor -- d’s",
# "bur -- nt", "my -- rrh", "len -- gth", "war -- mth". `y` counts as a vowel
# here, or "hó -- ly" would be swallowed whole.
s/ -- \([^aeiouyáéíóúýAEIOUYÁÉÍÓÚÝ ]*\)\>/\1/g;
# The same at the front: "sp -- read", "wh -- ole", "dw -- elling",
# "Ch -- riste", "B -- less", "m -- ale".
s/\<\([^aeiouyáéíóúýAEIOUYÁÉÍÓÚÝ ]*\) -- /\1/g;
# A silent -le(s) after a vowel: "rú -- les", "scá -- les", "í -- sles" are one
# syllable each. After a consonant it is a syllable and is left alone —
# "péo -- ples", "trém -- bles", "a -- pos -- tles". The alternation is what
# keeps "bláme -- less" out: `s*l[ée]s*` would have eaten its double s.
